// Cross-cutting infrastructure: feature flags, idempotency, rate limits (flags, rate-limit).

// --- Audit trail (Phase 8, issue #43) -------------------------------------
// One row per state transition on a record that matters for clinical,
// legal or financial accountability (PRD §7: "who, when"). Written by the
// audit module's record(), always inside the same transaction as the
// change it describes — if the change rolls back, so does its event.
//
// APPEND-ONLY. There is no update or delete path in code, and a database
// trigger (see the add_audit_event migration) rejects UPDATE, DELETE and
// TRUNCATE outright. Deliberately no foreign keys: an FK's ON DELETE action
// would have to rewrite or remove audit rows (which the trigger forbids),
// and RESTRICT would let the audit trail block account lifecycle. Ids are
// recorded as facts about the past.
//
// No PHI, ever: `metadata` is a small flat map of ids/codes validated by
// record() — never names, contact details, DOB, intake text, or copies of
// request/response bodies.
// Feature flags (Phase 8, issue #132): one row per flag, in this
// environment's own database (staging and production each have their own,
// so flags are per environment by construction). `enabled` is the kill
// switch: false = off for everyone. When true, a user is on if their id is
// in `allowUserIds`; otherwise they must be in `allowRoles` (when that list
// is non-empty) and fall inside the sticky `rolloutPercent` bucket. Changes
// are made only through the admin endpoints, which audit every one.
model FeatureFlag {
  key            String   @id
  description    String   @default("")
  enabled        Boolean  @default(false)
  rolloutPercent Int      @default(100)
  allowRoles     Role[]   @default([])
  allowUserIds   String[] @default([])
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime @updatedAt
}

// --- Idempotency (Phase 8, issue #48) ---------------------------------------
// One row per in-flight or completed `Idempotency-Key` request, so a client
// retrying after a mobile timeout gets the first response back instead of
// creating a second booking or a second payment.
//
// Scoped to (userId, key) — the unique constraint below is the whole
// concurrency mechanism. There is no in-memory lock anywhere: whoever wins
// the INSERT owns the attempt, everyone else reads the row. Two instances of
// this API therefore behave identically to one.
//
// `responseStatus`/`responseBody`/`responseHeaders` are NULL while the
// attempt is still running. That gap is what a concurrent duplicate sees, and
// what it waits on. It is NOT audit history: a 5xx deletes the row outright
// so the client can retry with the same key, and an attempt abandoned by a
// crashed process is taken over once it is older than the in-progress
// timeout. Rows are removed once `expiresAt` passes (24h by default).
//
// Deliberately NOT an `AuditEvent` and not a patient-data record: the cached
// body is a copy of a response the requesting user already received, kept
// only as long as a retry could still arrive, and it is never exported or
// listed. See ARCHITECTURE.md §4.12.
model IdempotencyKey {
  id              String   @id @default(uuid())
  // Scope: a key is only ever compared against the same user's other keys,
  // so one user can never replay (or read) another user's stored response.
  userId          String
  key             String
  method          String
  // Route pattern, not the URL: '/payments/intents/:id/fund'. Stored so a
  // row is self-describing when debugging, and folded into requestHash so a
  // key reused on a different route is refused rather than replayed.
  route           String
  // SHA-256 of the canonical request. Same key + same body -> replay;
  // same key + different body -> 422.
  requestHash     String
  responseStatus  Int?
  responseBody    Json?
  responseHeaders Json?
  createdAt       DateTime @default(now())
  expiresAt       DateTime

  @@unique([userId, key])
  @@index([expiresAt])
}

model RateLimitRecord {
  id         String   @id @default(uuid())
  key        String
  scope      String
  cost       Int      @default(1)
  consumedAt DateTime
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt  DateTime @updatedAt

  @@index([key, scope, consumedAt])
  @@index([consumedAt])
}
